  "toyota_summary": "Britain and China's trade chiefs discussed EV imports and Chinese investment in British factories this week, signaling London may diverge from the EU's strict tariff regime on Chinese vehicles. The EU is meanwhile considering a grace period until 2027 for automakers that can't meet new origin rules, essentially giving the industry three years to restructure supply chains before stricter enforcement kicks in. These parallel moves suggest a fracturing rulebook: manufacturers face potential tariff breaks in some markets while tariff walls harden in others, forcing them to choose between EU alignment and access to cheaper Chinese supply chains through Britain.\n\nThe stakes are immediate. Companies betting on British production gain leverage with Beijing but risk EU penalties. Those locked into European supply chains face forced restructuring at high cost if origin rules tighten as planned. The 2027 deadline is not a soft target—it's when the EU stops being lenient.\n\nWatch whether other countries follow Britain's lead on Chinese EV imports.",
